#!/usr/bin/env ruby
# frozen_string_literal: true

# bin/reviewer-select — previews the reviewer pair for a submitted task's PR under
# the Carl-owns-review model (docs/agents/agents/carl/sops/pr-review.md).
#
# **Carl is the STANDING PRIMARY** — the Lead Architect, deep reviewer, and OWNER
# of every PR review. There is no Avi supervisor. The review SESSION (a Pokémon
# orchestrator) claims reviewable PRs and spins ONE Carl per PR; each Carl runs
# the deep review and picks a LIGHT specialist at his discretion. This tool
# previews that pair — primary Carl + the domain-fit LIGHT — and records the review
# intent so the board shows the pair live.
#
# RECORDING IS THE DEFAULT. Producing the assignment also POSTs the picked pair as
# the `--to reviewed` review INTENT — an idempotent board write to
# /api/v1/tasks/<slug>/intent, the SAME endpoint `bin/task intent` uses — so
# /deployments + the task timeline show the reviewers LIVE (the crew ticker), before
# the →reviewed transition lands. Pass `--no-record` (or `--dry`) for advisory-only
# behavior (print the pick, write NOTHING). The board write is best-effort: a
# failure warns but never blocks the printed pick, and `--file` mode is always
# advisory (offline — nothing to write to).
#
# It loads the Rails app and calls `ReviewerSelector` for the task. The PRIMARY is
# always Carl; the LIGHT is a domain-fit pick from the specialist pool {shannon=UI
# · jasper=Web3 · steffon=DevOps/Platform · alex=Documentation}, scored by the
# task's shape + repositories + risk tags vs each soul's Agent.metadata["domains"]
# with a LOGGED, SEEDED-PER-TASK tiebreak (reproducible — not process-random). The
# QA owner (avi by default — after the 2026-07-22 reslot he runs qa-release + the QA
# deploy) is EXCLUDED from the light seat so one soul never both reviews AND QAs the
# same change. Because the tiebreak is seeded from the
# task identity, for the DEFAULT qa_owner this preview matches the pair the app
# records on the submitted→reviewed TaskEvent (a custom --qa-owner shifts the light
# pool + seed, so the preview is then advisory).
#
# Carl YIELDS the primary seat only to the hard no-self-review rule: if Carl is
# among the task's AUTHORS (not merely its last claimant) or a caller names him the
# qa_owner, he reviews nothing and BOTH seats become domain-fit picks from the
# specialist pool.
#
# Task source: the production task board API (same auth as bin/task / bin/dor-check),
# or a local JSON via --file (offline / tests). Degrades gracefully — a missing
# Agent row (or no DB at all) falls back to ReviewerSelector's domain defaults.
#
# EVERY AUTHOR is auto-excluded from the LIGHT seat, not just the last one to claim.
# A task can have several: a session limit kills a builder mid-work and another soul
# finishes it. devops.built_by holds ONE slug and RE-POINTS on a re-claim, so it
# names the current builder, not the author list — on 2026-08-30 that seated ALEX as
# the light on a diff Alex had written every test on (PR #1081, built_by=steffon),
# and a hand-passed `--busy alex` was the only thing that stopped it. The set is read
# from devops.builders (server-owned, append-only, stamped on every build claim),
# unioned with built_by and every `→ building` event actor EXCEPT a block's — a
# rework bounce lands the task back on `building`, so that transition names the
# REVIEWER who sent the work back, not an author.
#
# ⛔ THIS TOOL REFUSES (exit 2, selecting and recording NOTHING) in four states,
# because an empty exclusion list is not the same answer as "nobody to exclude" —
# conflating them is what let it pick Carl to review Carl's own PR on 2026-08-13:
#   1. NO author on record — nothing to exclude.
#   2. The author set is INCOMPLETE — another session claimed the task and named no
#      soul (devops.builders_unattributed), so a soul we cannot name worked on it.
#   3. An author would be SEATED — the pool was too small to drop them all, so one
#      was KEPT eligible (the recorder degrades here by design; the CLI must not).
#   4. A `--builder` entry NAMED NOBODY — see the roster note below.
# State the fact to proceed: `--builder <soul>[,<soul>]` names the authors,
# `--builder none` asserts that no soul built it. Fix it durably upstream with
# `bin/task move <slug> building --actor <soul>`.
#
# An UNRECOGNISED soul cannot lift any of that. Every slug is checked against the
# roster (Task.soul?), not merely a lowercase-word shape: `--builder stefon` names
# nobody, where it once passed as a known builder who excluded no one. A PARTIAL
# typo is the sharp case — `--builder steffon,alexx` yields a non-empty set, so the
# authors would read as KNOWN while the soul you meant to exclude never registered.
# That refuses too (state 4), rather than honoring the half it understood.
#
# BUSY souls (agents mid-build / mid-review on
# OTHER in-flight tasks) are excluded from the LIGHT seat via `--busy a,b,c` and/or
# `--busy-auto` (which reads the board's stage=building tasks). Both the author and
# the busy drops YIELD rather than starve: if the exclusions would leave too few
# light candidates, the least-bad ones are KEPT back so ReviewerSelector always
# returns a pair (flagged in the audit). The two kept cases are NOT equivalent here:
# a kept BUSY soul is an inconvenience and prints as a note, while a kept AUTHOR is
# a self-review and REFUSES (case 3 above).
#
# Usage:
#   bin/reviewer-select <task-slug>                 # print the pair AND record the review intent (default)
#   bin/reviewer-select <task-slug> --no-record     # advisory only — print the pick, record nothing
#   bin/reviewer-select <task-slug> --dry           # alias for --no-record
#   bin/reviewer-select <task-slug> --json          # machine-readable decision
#   bin/reviewer-select <task-slug> --qa-owner carl # exclude a different QA owner
#   bin/reviewer-select <task-slug> --builder carl        # override the recorded author set
#   bin/reviewer-select <task-slug> --builder steffon,alex # a task with TWO authors
#   bin/reviewer-select <task-slug> --builder none        # ASSERT no soul built it (lifts the refusal)
#   bin/reviewer-select <task-slug> --busy a,b      # exclude busy souls (repeatable)
#   bin/reviewer-select <task-slug> --busy-auto     # also exclude agents on stage=building tasks
#   bin/reviewer-select --file task.json            # evaluate a local task JSON (advisory — never records)
#
# Exit code: 0 = a pair was selected, 1 = task not found / usage error,
#            2 = REFUSED — the authors are unknown, incomplete, one would be
#                seated, or a --builder entry named nobody (see the block above).

# Activate the bundled gem versions first (Bundler.setup) — before requiring
# `json` et al, so the pinned json (not the newer default gem) is the one loaded.
# This is the same boot order bin/rails uses; config/environment (booted later,
# after the fetch) brings up the full app.
require_relative "../config/boot"
require "net/http"
require "json"
require "uri"
require "optparse"
require_relative "../lib/task_usage_baseline"
require_relative "../lib/task_usage_sandbox"
require_relative "lib/task_board"
require_relative "lib/session_identity"

ROOT = File.expand_path("..", __dir__)
BASE_URL = ENV.fetch("TASK_API_BASE", "https://mcritchie.studio")

# Seed the per-(session, slug) usage baseline at the review INTENT so the
# reviewer's submitted→reviewed move records a true token delta instead of a
# zeroed first-touch baseline (the model-only chip bug). Shares storage with
# bin/task — same dir resolution + JSON format — so the later `bin/task move
# reviewed` in the same session reads this baseline. Idempotent + best-effort:
# a failure never affects the printed reviewer pick.
def seed_review_usage_baseline(slug)
  session, provider = agent_session_identity
  return if session.empty? || slug.to_s.strip.empty?

  projects = (d = ENV["CLAUDE_PROJECTS_DIR"].to_s.strip).empty? ? File.expand_path("../..", __dir__) : File.expand_path(d)
  pinned = ENV["TASK_USAGE_DIR"].to_s.strip
  # Same live-store fallback bin/task carries, so it takes the same fail-closed
  # sandbox guard: under TASK_USAGE_SANDBOX an unpinned run aborts rather than
  # seeding a baseline into the operator's real cost store. The raw fallback is the
  # guard's ARGUMENT, never a local it is told about afterwards — see bin/task#usage_dir.
  dir = TaskUsageSandbox.enforce!(
    pinned.empty? ? File.join(projects, ".agents", "task-usage") : pinned,
    store: "task-usage"
  )
  TaskUsageBaseline.new(session: session, provider: provider, dir: dir).seed(slug)
rescue StandardError
  nil
end

def agent_session_identity
  id, provider = SessionIdentity.identity
  [id.to_s, provider || "claude"]
end

def warn!(msg)
  $stderr.puts(msg)
end

def die!(msg)
  warn!("error: #{msg}")
  exit 1
end

# --- task board auth + fetch (mirrors bin/task / bin/dor-check) ------------

def agent_secret
  dotenv = File.join(ROOT, ".env")
  TaskBoard.agent_secret(dotenv) ||
    die!("AGENT_API_SECRET not found (checked ENV, #{dotenv}, 1Password)")
end

def token
  @token ||= api(:post, "/api/v1/auth", { secret: agent_secret }, auth: false).fetch("token")
end

def api(method, path, body = nil, auth: true)
  res = TaskBoard.request(method, path, base_url: BASE_URL, token: (auth ? token : nil), body: body)
  parsed = TaskBoard.parse_body(res)
  die!("task not found") if method == :get && res.code.to_i == 404
  unless res.code.to_i.between?(200, 299)
    die!("#{method.upcase} #{path} -> #{res.code}: #{parsed["error"] || res.body}")
  end
  parsed
end

# Returns the task hash (slug + metadata.devops) from --file or the board API.
def load_task_json(slug, file)
  if file
    raw = JSON.parse(File.read(file))
    raw["data"] || raw
  else
    die!("usage: bin/reviewer-select <task-slug> [--json] [--qa-owner SLUG] | --file task.json") if slug.to_s.empty?
    res = api(:get, "/api/v1/tasks/#{slug}")
    res["data"] || die!("task #{slug} not found")
  end
rescue Errno::ENOENT
  die!("file not found: #{file}")
end

# The souls currently mid-build on OTHER tasks — every agent attributed to a
# stage=building task (its devops.built_by + agent_slug), minus the task we're
# selecting for. Best-effort: a board read failure (incl. api's die! → SystemExit)
# degrades to an empty set so --busy-auto can NEVER abort the pick — explicit
# --busy still applies.
#
# The fail-open is kept; its SILENCE is not. `Array(res["data"])` scored an
# unreadable body as "nobody is mid-build", which is the answer that hands a
# review to a soul already building elsewhere — and it did so with no signal at
# all, so the degradation was indistinguishable from a genuinely idle bench.
# `rows!` separates the two, and the rescue now SAYS which one happened before
# it degrades. Read it on stderr, so `--json` on stdout stays machine-clean.
def in_flight_busy(exclude_slug)
  res = api(:get, "/api/v1/tasks?stage=building")
  TaskBoard.rows!(res).reject { |t| t["slug"].to_s == exclude_slug.to_s }.flat_map do |t|
    [t.dig("metadata", "devops", "built_by"), t["agent_slug"]]
  end.map(&:to_s).reject(&:empty?).uniq
rescue SystemExit, StandardError => e
  warn!("busy-auto: could not read who is mid-build (#{e.class}: #{e.message.to_s.lines.first.to_s.strip}) — " \
        "proceeding with an EMPTY busy set. This is a DEGRADED pick, not an idle bench; " \
        "pass --busy <slugs> if you know who is occupied.")
  []
end

# Captures ReviewerSelector's structured tiebreak log line so the CLI can echo
# the audited roll instead of leaving it only in the Rails log.
class CaptureLogger
  attr_reader :lines

  def initialize
    @lines = []
  end

  def info(msg = nil)
    @lines << (msg || (block_given? ? yield : ""))
  end
end

# --- human-readable output ------------------------------------------------

def fmt_list(list)
  arr = Array(list)
  arr.empty? ? "-" : arr.join(", ")
end

def seat_line(label, seat)
  matched = seat["matched"].empty? ? "(no domain match — seeded tiebreak)" : seat["matched"].join(", ")
  format("%-5s  %-10s  matched: %-30s  (fit %d, roll %.4f)",
         label, seat["slug"], matched, seat["fit"], seat["roll"])
end

# The auditable "who's out" line of the LIGHT pool: the QA owner always, plus the
# builder when known — flagged "(builder — excluded)" when removed from the light
# pool, "(builder — KEPT: …)" when kept because excluding it would starve the pool,
# or "(builder — not a light candidate)" when the builder was never eligible for
# the light seat (Carl the standing primary, a non-specialist, or the QA owner) —
# plus any busy souls (excluded, or KEPT when excluding them would have starved).
def excluded_line(decision)
  parts = ["#{decision["excluded_qa_owner"]} (QA owner — never reviews a PR he then QAs)"]
  builder = decision["builder"].to_s
  # A known-but-nameless builder is the `--builder none` assertion: nobody is
  # excluded, and the audit line says WHY that is a fact rather than a blank.
  #
  # KEY ON THE ASSERTION, NOT ON A PROXY FOR IT. This read `builder.empty? &&
  # builder_known`, which was a sound restatement of "the caller said none" only
  # while builder_known? was defined over the SINGULAR builder. Once it was
  # redefined over the author SET, the two stopped agreeing: `builders` also unions
  # devops.builders, which the singular #builder never reads. A task whose authors
  # come from that key alone then printed BOTH "no builder — ASSERTED by the
  # caller" and the author rows below it — self-contradicting, and claiming the
  # operator stated a fact they never stated.
  parts << "(no builder — ASSERTED by the caller: no soul built this task)" if decision["builder_asserted_none"]
  # EVERY author, not just the current builder. A task can have several (a session
  # limit kills a builder mid-work and another soul finishes it), and printing only
  # devops.built_by is how an author read as a free reviewer.
  excluded_builders = Array(decision["excluded_builders"])
  kept_builders = Array(decision["kept_builders"])
  Array(decision["builders"]).each do |soul|
    parts << if excluded_builders.include?(soul)
      "#{soul} (author — a soul never reviews their own work)"
    elsif kept_builders.include?(soul)
      "#{soul} (author — KEPT: excluding would leave too few candidates)"
    else
      "#{soul} (author — not a light candidate; reviews nothing on this PR)"
    end
  end
  Array(decision["excluded_busy"]).each { |s| parts << "#{s} (busy — mid-build/review elsewhere)" }
  Array(decision["kept_busy"]).each { |s| parts << "#{s} (busy — KEPT: excluding would leave too few candidates)" }
  parts.join("; ")
end

def print_human(decision, pr_url, log_line)
  primary, light = decision["reviewers"]
  puts "reviewer-select  task=#{decision["task"] || "-"}  shape=#{decision["shape"] || "-"}  " \
       "repos=#{fmt_list(decision["repositories"])}  risks=#{fmt_list(decision["risk_tags"])}"
  puts "needs-domains:   #{fmt_list(decision["needed_domains"])}"
  puts "excluded:        #{excluded_line(decision)}"
  puts
  # The seat labels echo the actual role names the decision carries (sourced from
  # the vocabulary via ReviewerSelector), so the CLI never hardcodes the role.
  puts seat_line(primary["weight"].to_s.upcase, primary)
  puts seat_line(light["weight"].to_s.upcase, light)
  puts
  puts "tiebreak (auditable — also logged to Rails.logger):"
  puts "  candidates: #{decision["candidates"].join(", ")}"
  puts "  rolls:      #{decision["ranked"].map { |c| "#{c["slug"]}:#{format("%.4f", c["roll"])}" }.join("  ")}"
  puts "  ranked:     #{decision["ranked"].map { |c| "#{c["slug"]}(fit#{c["fit"]})" }.join(" > ")}"
  puts "  chosen:     #{primary["slug"]} (#{primary["weight"]}) + #{light["slug"]} (#{light["weight"]})"
  puts "  log:        #{log_line}" if log_line
  puts
  if pr_url.empty?
    puts "PR:              (none yet — selection is advisory until the PR is opened)"
  else
    puts "PR:              #{pr_url}"
  end
end

# --- main -----------------------------------------------------------------

# record defaults to TRUE — selecting the pair also writes the review intent so the
# deploy-lane crew ticker shows them live. --no-record / --dry opt back out.
options = { json: false, file: nil, qa_owner: nil, builder: nil, busy: [], busy_auto: false, record: true }
OptionParser.new do |o|
  o.banner = "Usage: bin/reviewer-select <task-slug> [--json] [--no-record|--dry] [--qa-owner SLUG] [--builder SLUG] [--busy a,b,c] [--busy-auto] | bin/reviewer-select --file task.json"
  o.on("--json", "Emit a machine-readable decision (single-line JSON)") { options[:json] = true }
  # --[no-]record gives both forms: --record (the default; kept as a back-compat
  # no-op) and --no-record (opt out of the board write — advisory only).
  o.on("--[no-]record", "Record (DEFAULT) / don't record the picked pair as a review INTENT on the task " \
                        "(board write) so the board + timeline show them reviewing live, before →reviewed lands") do |v|
    options[:record] = v
  end
  o.on("--dry", "--dry-run", "Advisory only — print the pick, record NOTHING (alias for --no-record)") { options[:record] = false }
  o.on("--qa-owner SLUG", "Exclude this slug instead of the default QA owner (avi)") { |v| options[:qa_owner] = v }
  o.on("--builder SLUG", "Exclude these authors, comma/space separated (overrides the task's recorded set); " \
                         "`none` ASSERTS that no soul built the task, lifting the unknown-author refusal") do |v|
    options[:builder] = v
  end
  o.on("--busy LIST", "Exclude these busy souls — mid-build/review elsewhere (comma/space separated; repeatable)") do |v|
    options[:busy].concat(v.split(/[\s,]+/))
  end
  o.on("--busy-auto", "Also treat agents on stage=building tasks as busy (board query; skipped with --file)") { options[:busy_auto] = true }
  o.on("--file PATH", "Evaluate a local task JSON instead of fetching from the board") { |v| options[:file] = v }
  o.on("-h", "--help", "Show this help") do
    puts o
    exit 0
  end
end.parse!(ARGV)

slug = ARGV.shift
task_json = load_task_json(slug, options[:file])

# Boot the Rails app — ReviewerSelector reads Agent metadata + uses the Task model.
require_relative "../config/environment"

devops = task_json.dig("metadata", "devops") || {}
task = Task.new(slug: task_json["slug"], metadata: { "devops" => devops })
qa_owner = options[:qa_owner].to_s.strip
qa_owner = ReviewerSelector::DEFAULT_QA_OWNER if qa_owner.empty?
builder = options[:builder].to_s.strip
builder = nil if builder.empty?

# The busy set: explicit --busy souls, plus (with --busy-auto, board-only) the
# agents on OTHER stage=building tasks. The selector excludes them from review but
# never starves the pool below a PRIMARY+LIGHT pair (least-bad kept back).
busy = options[:busy].map { |s| s.to_s.strip }.reject(&:empty?).uniq
if options[:busy_auto]
  if options[:file]
    warn!("--busy-auto needs the board (skipped in --file mode)")
  elsif slug.to_s.empty?
    warn!("--busy-auto needs a task slug")
  else
    busy = (busy + in_flight_busy(slug)).uniq
  end
end

log = CaptureLogger.new
decision = ReviewerSelector.new(task, qa_owner: qa_owner, builder: builder, busy: busy, logger: log).decision
pr_url = devops["pr_url"].to_s.strip

# FAIL CLOSED ON AN UNKNOWN BUILDER. Selection exists to keep a soul off the
# review of their own PR; with no builder on record it cannot do that, and the
# old behavior — print `builder=-` and roll a reviewer anyway — read like ordinary
# output rather than a disabled safety check. On 2026-08-13 it picked Carl to
# review Carl's own PR, and a human noticing was the only thing that stopped it.
#
# So an absent fact REFUSES rather than defaulting to the permissive answer, and
# it refuses BEFORE the intent write — a blind pair must never reach the board.
# Two ways out, both stating a fact rather than omitting one:
#   --builder <soul>  name who built it (also the after-the-fact correction)
#   --builder none    assert NO soul built it (a Pokémon session, an operator)
# The durable fix is upstream: `bin/task move <slug> building --actor <soul>`
# stamps devops.built_by on any build claim, re-claims included.
# Prefer the positional slug, but fall back to the one the payload carries so
# --file mode prints a copy-pasteable remedy too.
named = slug.to_s.strip.empty? ? decision["task"].to_s.strip : slug.to_s.strip
named = "<task>" if named.empty?

# A PARTLY understood override is refused before the known/unknown question: with
# `--builder steffon,stefon` the set is non-empty, so the authors read as KNOWN
# while the soul the caller meant to exclude never registered.
if (unresolved = Array(decision["builder_override_unresolved"])).any?
  warn!("reviewer-select REFUSED to select for task=#{decision["task"] || "-"} — AN AUTHOR NAMED NOBODY.")
  warn!("  #{unresolved.join(", ")} matches no soul on the roster, so #{unresolved.size == 1 ? "it names" : "they name"} nobody and")
  warn!("  #{unresolved.size == 1 ? "excludes" : "exclude"} nobody. The rest of your list would still have been honored,")
  warn!("  which is how a typo hides inside a correct answer. Check the spelling against:")
  warn!("    #{ReviewerSelector::POOL.union([ReviewerSelector::DEFAULT_QA_OWNER]).sort.join(", ")}")
  exit 2
end

unless decision["builder_known"]
  warn!("reviewer-select REFUSED to select for task=#{decision["task"] || "-"} — the AUTHORS ARE UNKNOWN.")
  if builder
    # An override was passed and it named NOBODY on the roster — a typo. Say that,
    # rather than the record's story: `--builder stefon` is a different problem from
    # an unstamped task, and reporting the wrong one sends the reader to fix the
    # wrong thing.
    warn!("  --builder #{builder.inspect} matches no soul on the roster, and an explicit")
    warn!("  override is authoritative — so it names nobody rather than falling back to")
    warn!("  the record it was meant to correct. Check the spelling against:")
    warn!("    #{ReviewerSelector::POOL.union([ReviewerSelector::DEFAULT_QA_OWNER]).sort.join(', ')}")
  elsif (session = decision["builders_unattributed"].to_s.strip).empty?
    if (stray = Array(decision["record_unresolved"])).any?
      # NAMED, BUT NOT A SOUL. Saying "blank" here is false, and the durable fix
      # below would overwrite the typo rather than correct it — so the reader is
      # told what the record actually holds.
      warn!("  The record NAMES #{stray.join(", ")}, which matches no soul on the roster,")
      warn!("  so the author set is empty even though devops.built_by is NOT blank. The")
      warn!("  exclusion has nothing to exclude. Check the spelling against:")
      warn!("    #{ReviewerSelector::POOL.union([ReviewerSelector::DEFAULT_QA_OWNER]).sort.join(", ")}")
    else
      warn!("  devops.built_by is blank and no `→ building` BUILD CLAIM names a soul, so")
      warn!("  the no-self-review exclusion has nothing to exclude. Selecting anyway could")
      warn!("  hand the PR to the soul who wrote it — refusing instead.")
      warn!("  (A rework block bounces the task onto `building` too. That transition is the")
      warn!("  REVIEWER's and is deliberately NOT read as authorship, so after a bounce the")
      warn!("  trail holds no author to find — state the fact, or stamp it durably below.)")
    end
  else
    # The INCOMPLETE set. Someone else worked this task and the record cannot say
    # who — so the authors on record are a subset, and a pick made over that subset
    # can still land on an author (measured 2026-08-30: PR #1081 seated Alex).
    warn!("  Authors on record: #{Array(decision["builders"]).join(", ")}.")
    warn!("  But session #{session} ALSO worked this task — it claimed or shipped it")
    warn!("  while naming no soul — so the author list is INCOMPLETE, and the exclusion")
    warn!("  cannot cover a soul it cannot name. A handoff mid-build does this: a")
    warn!("  session limit kills the claimer and ANOTHER soul writes and ships the diff,")
    warn!("  so the set names the claimer and the real author is still in the pool.")
  end
  warn!("")
  warn!("  Resolve it by stating the fact (name EVERY author, comma-separated):")
  warn!("    bin/reviewer-select #{named} --builder <soul>[,<soul>]   # who wrote it")
  warn!("    bin/reviewer-select #{named} --builder none              # no soul built it")
  warn!("  Durable fix (stamps the author for next time, re-claims included):")
  warn!("    bin/task move #{named} building --actor <soul>")
  exit 2
end

# THE SECOND WAY AN AUTHOR GETS SEATED: the pool was too small to drop them all.
# `builders`/`busy` exclusions YIELD rather than starve, so the selector always
# returns a pair — correct for the model recorder, which must never break. It is
# NOT correct here, where a human is present to choose: a kept author is a soul
# about to review their own diff, and printing it as a note (which is all this did)
# is the same "reads like ordinary output" failure as the blank builder in the
# 2026-08-13 incident. The CLI fails closed; the recorder still degrades.
if (kept = Array(decision["kept_builders"])).any?
  warn!("reviewer-select REFUSED to select for task=#{decision["task"] || "-"} — AN AUTHOR WOULD BE SEATED.")
  warn!("  Authors: #{Array(decision["builders"]).join(", ")}. Excluding #{kept.join(", ")} too would")
  warn!("  leave too few light candidates, so the pool KEPT them eligible — and the")
  warn!("  light seat may land on a soul who wrote the diff. Refusing instead.")
  warn!("")
  # NO FLAG WIDENS THIS, and the one printed here made it WORSE. `--qa-owner` was
  # offered as "free the QA-owner seat", but there is no seat to free: the light
  # pool is POOL minus the standing primary, and the default QA owner is not in
  # POOL at all — so the pool is ALREADY maximal. Every override holds or shrinks
  # it. Measured on a four-author fixture, `--qa-owner carl` costs the
  # standing-primary seat, raising the minimum the pool must keep from 1 to 2 and
  # taking kept authors from 1 to 2 — strictly worse than doing nothing. A remedy
  # that moves the reader backwards is worse than none, because the reader who
  # cannot act on a refusal routes around it, and the route around here is
  # `--builder none`, which lifts the guard entirely.
  warn!("  NO FLAG WIDENS THIS. The light pool is already at its maximum:")
  warn!("    #{(ReviewerSelector::POOL - [ReviewerSelector::STANDING_PRIMARY]).join(", ")}")
  warn!("  --qa-owner cannot enlarge it (#{ReviewerSelector::DEFAULT_QA_OWNER} is not in the pool, so no")
  warn!("  seat is held); naming any light REMOVES them, and naming #{ReviewerSelector::STANDING_PRIMARY} costs the")
  warn!("  standing-primary seat and raises the minimum the pool must keep.")
  warn!("")
  warn!("  If the author list OVER-NAMES (a stale stamp, a soul who only rebased),")
  warn!("  state the true set — this is the only lever that clears the refusal:")
  warn!("    bin/reviewer-select #{named} --builder <soul>[,<soul>]   # who ACTUALLY wrote it")
  warn!("  If it is correct, every eligible light authored this diff. There is no")
  warn!("  eligible light: hand the seat to a soul outside the pool, or split the PR.")
  exit 2
end

# Record (the DEFAULT) the picked pair onto the task as a review INTENT (a board
# write) so /deployments + the task timeline show the two seniors reviewing live,
# with a ticking timer, the moment Avi kicks off the review — before the →reviewed
# transition lands. Hits the SAME idempotent endpoint as `bin/task intent` (an
# identical current-cycle intent is reused; a resubmitted rework task can record a
# fresh review intent). Best-effort: a board-write
# failure (incl. api's die! → SystemExit) WARNS but never blocks the printed pick
# — the conductor always gets the assignment. --file mode is offline → always
# advisory.
recorded = nil
if options[:record]
  if options[:file]
    warn!("intent not recorded — --file mode is offline (selection is advisory)")
  elsif slug.to_s.empty?
    warn!("intent not recorded — no task slug (selection is advisory)")
  else
    primary, light = decision["reviewers"]
    pair = [{ "slug" => primary["slug"], "weight" => primary["weight"] },
            { "slug" => light["slug"], "weight" => light["weight"] }]
    begin
      api(:post, "/api/v1/tasks/#{slug}/intent",
          { to_stage: "reviewed", reviewers: pair, event: { source: "cli" } })
      recorded = pair
      seed_review_usage_baseline(slug)
    rescue SystemExit, StandardError => e
      warn!("intent not recorded — board write failed (#{e.message}); selection is advisory")
    end
  end
end

if options[:json]
  puts JSON.generate(decision.merge("pr_url" => pr_url.empty? ? nil : pr_url, "intent_recorded" => !recorded.nil?))
else
  print_human(decision, pr_url, log.lines.last)
  puts "intent:          recorded (#{recorded.map { |r| "#{r["slug"]}/#{r["weight"]}" }.join(' + ')}) — they now show reviewing live" if recorded
end
exit 0
